{#
    `.. example::` — the source, and under it that source rendered.

    The template that draws every other code block draws this one. So it gets
    its colour on the server and carries the copy button. What a reader takes
    away is the body that produced the thing below it.

    `.sds-example` is the frame: dashed and unfilled. That is what says the box
    is not part of the page, and leaves what is inside it on the ground it
    really stands on. Nothing new — the class layer draws it.

    The blocks stand in it directly. They carry their own step, and the frame
    takes the last one off. A container between them states one distance for
    a heading and a paragraph alike, and mostly wraps a single child.
#}
{{ renderNode(node.source) }}
<div class="sds-example{% if node.option('class') %} {{ node.option('class') }}{% endif %}">
    {%- for child in node.children -%}
        {{ renderNode(child) }}
    {%- endfor -%}
</div>
